On the default question =E2=80=94 I would put it on the base class defaulting=
 to off. The silent failure you are describing is real, but it is not symmetr=
ic with the current one. Under the if-chain, the eligibility fact is invisibl=
e from the provider entirely, so there is nothing to review; under a flag wit=
h a default, it is a missing line in a class where every other pricing attrib=
ute is sitting in plain sight, and that is the kind of omission a reader noti=
ces. Defaulting to off also means the failure mode is that we overcharge ours=
elves in an estimate rather than undercharge, which is the direction I would =
rather be wrong in.

So: flag on the base class, off by default, each provider that actually offer=
s batch pricing sets it true alongside its other rates, and batch_multiplier =
does a lookup instead of a name comparison. I am not entirely sure whether we=
 want the discount rate itself on the class as well, but that is a separate a=
rgument and the current numbers do not force it.

That said, I do not think the if-chain was a mistake when it was written. Wit=
h two providers in it, it read fine. It stopped reading fine somewhere around=
 the fourth, and none of us were looking at it on the day that happened.
